There is an absolute constant $c>0$ such that, for every fixed choice of integers $a_1,a_2\ge1$ and $b_1,b_2\ge0$ with $a_1b_2-a_2b_1\ne0$, there is a constant $C_{a_1,a_2,b_1,b_2}$ for which $$\left|\sum_{1\le n\le X}
       \lambda(a_1n+b_1)\lambda(a_2n+b_2)\right|
 \le C_{a_1,a_2,b_1,b_2}\frac{X}{(\log X)^c}
 \qquad(X\ge3).$$ The cutoff $X$ may be any real number. No coprimality condition is imposed on the coefficients.

There is one absolute $c>0$ such that, for every fixed $h,l\ge1$ and $b\in\mathbb Z$, $$F(X):=\frac1X\sum_{1\le n\le X}
   \mathbf 1_{n\equiv b\pmod l}\lambda(n)\lambda(n+h)
 \ll_{h,l}(\log X)^{-c}\qquad(X\ge3).$$ The estimate holds for every real $X$, with no condition on $\gcd(b,l)$.

There is an absolute constant $C_1$ with the following property. Choose $A>C_1+20$. Let $l\ge1$ be fixed, and let $\mathcal R$ be any set of primes at most $e^L$, none dividing $l$. On the coordinates modulo $p\in\mathcal R$ and modulo $l$, let $\mu$ be the product of the uniform laws; the modulus-one coordinate can be omitted. Suppose that an event $\mathcal E$ is represented by a circuit of depth at most $20$ and size at most $\exp(L^6)$ in these residue equalities. For every consecutive integer interval $I$ with $|I|\ge\exp(L^A/2)$, $$\label{q:finite-law-bound}
 \bigl|\mathbb E_I\mathbf 1_{\mathcal E}-\mu(\mathcal E)\bigr|
       \le e^{-L^{10}}$$ for sufficiently large $L$ depending on $l$. The bound is uniform in the position of $I$ and in all offsets used by the tests. The modulus $l$ need not be squarefree.

For every offset $a$ and every interval $I$ allowed in [q:finite-law](#q:finite-law), $$\label{q:truncated-weight-average}
 \mathbb E_I\bigl[w(n+a)\mathbf 1_{\omega_Q(n+a)\le400\log L}\bigr]
                    \le S+e^{-L^9}\le2S$$ for sufficiently large $L$. The analogous sum over $M$ fixed offsets is at most $2MS$.

For every pair of real numbers $10\le H\le Z$, $$\sup_{\alpha\in\mathbb R}
 \int_0^Z
 \left|\sum_{x\le n\le x+H}\lambda(n)\mathrm e(\alpha n)\right|\,dx
 \ll HZ\left(\frac{\log\log H}{\log H}
                  +(\log Z)^{-1/700}\right),$$ with an absolute implied constant. The supremum is outside the integral.

Fix integers $h,l\ge1$ and $b_0\in\mathbb Z$. Let $A\ge1000$, let $L$ be sufficiently large, and suppose $Y\ge\exp(L^A/2)$. Let $D$ be a positive integer and let $\mathcal Z\subset[D,2D)$ satisfy the assumptions of Lemma <a href="#q:rough-fourier" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="q:rough-fourier">10</a>. Then $$\label{q:rough-correlation}
 \left|\frac1Y\sum_{1\le v\le Y}
       \sum_{z\in\mathcal Z}\frac1z
       \mathbf 1_{v\equiv b_0\pmod l}\lambda(v)\lambda(v+hz)\right|
 \ll_{h,l}L^{-21/20}.$$

Suppose the family $\mathcal B_j$ satisfies the covariance, probability, and circuit hypotheses just stated. Then $$\label{q:cuts-bound}
 \frac1{SV_*}\sum_j|C_j-C_j^\circ|
 \ll_{h,l}
 2^J\bigl(K^{-1}+L^{-100}+e^{-2WJ}\bigr)
       +\exp(-L^{0.9}).$$ This estimate holds for the integer averages defining $C_j$ and $C_j^\circ$, for every sufficiently large real $X$.

There is an absolute constant $C_2$ such that $$\label{q:trace-bound}
 \mathbb E\|H^k\|_{\mathrm{HS}}^2
 \le\bigl[K(C_2\sqrt W)^J\bigr]^{2k}.$$ The norm is the unnormalized Hilbert–Schmidt norm. The same estimate, after increasing $C_2$ absolutely, holds when block origins are averaged over any integer interval of length at least $\exp(\tfrac12L^A)$. The threshold for $L$ may depend on the fixed data.

The absolute expectation of a designated term of <a href="#q:word-product" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="q:word-product">[q:word-product]</a> is at most $$\label{q:mixed-majorant}
 \mathbf 1_{\mathrm{lit\ consistency}}\,b_{\mathcal L}
       \mathbb E\bigl[R|\Delta G|\bigr].$$ In particular $|\Delta G|\le2^{S_1}$.

Fix an integer $m\ge1$, a bin $j$, tuples $d_1,\ldots,d_m\in\mathcal D$, signs $\varepsilon_1,\ldots,\varepsilon_m\in\{-1,1\}$, an initial site, and the entire non-$P$ residue environment. With successive sites defined by $n_{i+1}=n_i+\varepsilon_i hq_i d_i$, one has $$\label{q:padding-product}
 \sum_{q_1,\ldots,q_m\in\mathcal Q}
 \prod_{i=1}^m
 \frac{Lu(q_i)}{w(n_i)}\mathbf 1_{q_i\mid n_i}
 \mathbf 1_{(d_i,q_i)\in\mathcal S_j}
 \mathbf 1_{\rho_j(n_i,d_i)\le K/L}
 \le K^m.$$ The bound is uniform over the fixed data and initial site.

On every block, in either the product model or the integer model, $$\begin{aligned}
 \|A_d\|&\le2K,\label{q:single-operator}\\
 \sum_{d\in\mathcal D}\|A_d v\|^2
 &\le4K^2(8W)^J\|v\|^2\qquad(v=Ev).
 \label{q:square-operators}
\end{aligned}$$

There is an absolute constant $C_3$ with the following property. For each bin $j$, average block origins over any integer interval to which [q:trace](#q:trace) applies. Outside a fraction at most $e^{-2k}$ of these origins, $$\label{q:compressed-norm}
 \left\|E\left(\sum_d A_d\right)E\right\|
 \le K(C_3\sqrt W)^J.$$

Fix $h\ge1$, $1<\tau<2$, $C_0\ge1$, and $T>0$, and use <a href="#eq:graph-constants" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:graph-constants">[eq:graph-constants]</a>–<a href="#eq:graph-weights" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:graph-weights">[eq:graph-weights]</a>. For every sufficiently large $B$, every admissible family $\mathcal D$, all permitted cutoffs $w_d$, all coefficients $|a_d|\le1$, and all functions $F,G:\mathbb N\to\mathbb D$, $$\label{eq:graph-estimate}
 \limsup_{X\to\infty}\frac1X
 \left|\sum_{1\le x\le X}\sum_{d\in\mathcal D}
 a_dF(x)G(x+hd)g_d(x,x+hd)\right|
 \ll L_0 B^{-1-c_*/2}.$$ The implied constant and the threshold for $B$ may depend on $h,\tau,C_0,T$, and the fixed constants in <a href="#eq:graph-constants" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:graph-constants">[eq:graph-constants]</a>, but not on $H,\mathcal D,w_d,a_d,F,G$. All graph parameters are fixed when $X$ tends to infinity.

Let $f_1,f_2\colon\mathbb N\to\mathbb C$ be $1$-bounded multiplicative functions, with $f_1(1)=f_2(1)=1$, satisfying <a href="#eq:analytic-bias" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:analytic-bias">[eq:analytic-bias]</a> and <a href="#eq:analytic-finite-defects" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:analytic-finite-defects">[eq:analytic-finite-defects]</a>. Choose $1<\tau<2$ and $T>0$ with $\tau-1\le\gamma/64$ and $32/T^2\le\gamma/64$, and use a cutoff $\phi$ as in <a href="#eq:analytic-cutoffs" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:analytic-cutoffs">[eq:analytic-cutoffs]</a>, equal to $1$ on $[-T/2,T/2]$. With $\mathcal D,H$ supplied by [lem:raw-bin](#lem:raw-bin), set $$a_d=\overline{f_1(d)f_2(d)},\qquad X_j=HN_j.$$ There is $c_\gamma>0$, independent of $B,j$, such that, for every sufficiently large fixed $B$, $$\label{eq:raw-lower-bound}
 \liminf_{j\to\infty}\frac{|R_B(X_j)|}{X_j}
 \ge c_\gamma\frac{L_0}{B}.$$

Let $f_1,f_2\colon\mathbb N\to\mathbb C$ be multiplicative, with $|f_i|\le1$, and suppose at least one is uniformly nonpretentious. Fix $h\ge1$, $1<\tau<2$, $C_0\ge1$, $T>0$, and a smooth function $0\le\phi\le1$ supported in $[-T,T]$. For each sufficiently large $B$, let $\mathcal D,H,J$ satisfy <a href="#eq:divisor-family" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:divisor-family">[eq:divisor-family]</a>, let $|a_d|\le1$, and use <a href="#eq:analytic-cutoffs" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:analytic-cutoffs">[eq:analytic-cutoffs]</a>. Define $R_B,C_B$ by <a href="#eq:raw-sum" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:raw-sum">[eq:raw-sum]</a> and <a href="#eq:analytic-centered-sum" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:analytic-centered-sum">[eq:analytic-centered-sum]</a>. Then $$\label{eq:analytic-centering-bound}
 \limsup_{X\to\infty}\frac{|R_B(X)-C_B(X)|}{X}
 \ll L_0\left(B^{-1-\varepsilon}+\frac{J}{P_0}\right).$$ The implied constant may depend on the fixed parameters and $\phi$, but is independent of the divisor family and coefficients. The long-variable limit is taken with $B$ fixed.

Let $f,b\colon\mathbb N\to\mathbb C$ be multiplicative and $1$-bounded. Suppose their prime values agree outside a finite set $\mathcal P$. For every Dirichlet character $\chi$, every real $t$, and $X\ge2$, $$\label{eq:analytic-distance-stability}
 \left|D(b,\chi n^{it};X)^2-D(f,\chi n^{it};X)^2\right|
 \le2\sum_{p\in\mathcal P}\frac1p.$$ Consequently, if $f$ is uniformly nonpretentious, the same divergence holds uniformly over all such $b$ and over every fixed finite family of Dirichlet characters.

Let $X\ge D\ge10$ and let $b\colon\mathbb N\to\mathbb C$ be a $1$-bounded multiplicative function. With $Q=\min((\log X)^{1/125},(\log D)^5)$, one has $$\label{eq:mrt-input}
 \begin{split}
 \sup_{\alpha\in\mathbb R/\mathbb Z}\int_0^X
 \left|\sum_{y<m\le y+D}b(m)\mathrm e(\alpha m)\right|\,dy
 \ll DX\left(
 e^{-\mathcal M(b;X,Q)/20}
 +\frac{\log\log D}{\log D}
 +\frac1{(\log X)^{1/700}}\right).
 \end{split}$$ The implied constant is absolute.

For the divisor family, cutoffs, and kernels of [sec:graph-setup](#sec:graph-setup), the periodic set $Y$ in [def:primitive-specification](#def:primitive-specification) satisfies, as $B\to\infty$, $$\begin{aligned}
 \mathbb P(n\notin Y)&\le P_0^{-1+o(1)},\label{eq:deleted-density}\\
 \sum_{\boldsymbol d}
 \left|\mathbb EK_{\boldsymbol d}(n)
       \prod_{j=0}^{\ell}\mathbf 1_Y(n+b_j)\right|
   &\le B^{-(1+c_*)\ell}.\label{eq:high-trace}
\end{aligned}$$ The sum is over all closed lines of length $\ell$. The asymptotic bounds are uniform over the admissible divisor families and cutoffs, with $h,\tau,C_0,T$ fixed.

Consider a line of length $\ell$ together with at most $2t_0$ attached qualifying specifications, each of length at most $L$. Refine its slot pattern by recording the lit or unlit status of every line occurrence. There are $\exp(O(B\log^2B))$ slot patterns, including signs, attachments, occurrence statuses, and recorded vertex identifications. For each assigned pattern and its numerical prime values, let $\mathfrak A$ be its attached specifications and let $E$ be any residue event imposing the recorded occurrence statuses, possibly with further restrictions. Then $$\label{eq:absolute-pattern-majorant}
 \mathbb E\left[|K_{\boldsymbol d}(n)|\mathbf 1_E(n)
     \prod_{\sigma\in\mathfrak A}
       \mathbf 1_{\{\sigma\text{ qualifies at its assigned vertex}\}}\right]
 \le A^{\ell J}\prod_{p\text{ used}}\frac1p.$$ If the recorded statuses include at least $t_0$ unlit occurrences among center primes occurring at least twice on the line, this majorant has the additional factor $P_0^{-t_0/2}$. The total contribution of that class, summed over all patterns and numerical assignments, is $O(\exp(-B^{1+\varepsilon/2}))$.

Suppose that the cylinders in $\mathscr E$ have width at most $w\ge1$. For an integer $t\ge1$, put $M=tw$, and let $$\mathscr H_t=\{J\in\mathscr P:\operatorname{rk}(J)\ge t,
       \ J\text{ is generated by at most }t\text{ members of }\mathscr E\}.$$ Then the rank is nondecreasing on $\mathscr P$, every $I\in\mathscr P$ is generated by at most $\operatorname{rk}(I)$ events, and $$\label{eq:cylinder-width}
 |\operatorname{supp}(I)|\le w\operatorname{rk}(I).$$ For every $x\in\Omega$, $$\label{eq:cylinder-truncation}
 \left|\mathbf 1_{x\notin\bigcup_{E\in\mathscr E}E}
       -\sum_{\operatorname{rk}(I)<t}\mu(\widehat0,I)\mathbf 1_I(x)\right|
 \le 2^M\max(1,M^{M+1})\sum_{J\in\mathscr H_t}\mathbf 1_J(x).$$ Every coefficient retained in the sum on the left has absolute value at most $\max(1,M^{M+1})$. Every intersection on the right has width at most $M$, even if its rank is greater than $t$.

For the retained line and fixed-coordinate data of [prop:retained-lines](#prop:retained-lines), there is a factor $\mathfrak C_B=\exp(O(M_0\log B))$ such that $$\begin{aligned}
 &\sum_{\boldsymbol d}\mathbb E_{\mathcal F}
   \left[R_{\boldsymbol d}
     \left|\mathbb E_{\mathcal S\setminus\mathcal F}
          K_{\boldsymbol d}(n)\prod_{j=0}^{\ell}\mathbf 1_Y(n+b_j)\right|\right]
       \notag\\
 &\quad\le \mathfrak C_B\sum_{\boldsymbol d}\mathbb E_{\mathcal F}
   \left[R_{\boldsymbol d}
     \sum_{\mathfrak L\in\mathscr L_{<t_0}(\boldsymbol d)}
       \left|\mathbb E_{\mathcal S\setminus\mathcal F}
                      K_{\boldsymbol d}(n)I_{\mathfrak L}(n)\right|\right]
       +O\!\left(e^{-B^{1+\varepsilon/2}}\right).
       \label{eq:cylinder-reduction}
\end{aligned}$$ Here each sum runs over numerical closed lines, $\mathcal F$ is the fixed prime set of that line, and every list indicator imposes full qualification of its specifications.

For a closed line of length $\ell$ with $r_*$ returns, the topology of first visits and returns, together with all step signs, has at most $4^\ell(\ell+1)^{r_*}$ possibilities. Its tree satisfies $$\label{eq:trace-bad-edges}
 |E(\mathscr T)\setminus\mathcal G|\le 10(r_*+1),\qquad
 \ell=|E(\mathscr T)|+r_*.$$ Once the labels of the tree edges and this topology are specified, every return label is determined.

Uniformly over retained lines, lists and assigned records, $$\begin{aligned}
 \mathcal I(R)
 &\le
 \exp\left\{
 o(1)-\kappa(v_C-T\sqrt{v_C})(r_*+|\mathcal U|)\right\}
 \notag\\
 &\quad\times\exp\left\{
 -v_CS_C(\mathscr T)-v_ZS_Z(\mathscr T)
 +v_C\beta_C(e^\kappa-1)|\mathcal U|\right\}
 \notag\\
 &\quad\times\prod_{\substack{p\text{ distinct}\\
                  p\text{ in tree or list}}}\frac1p
 \prod_{\text{tagged tokens}}\mathrm{weight}
 \prod_{\substack{\text{untagged tokens}\\(a,Q)}}\widetilde u_a(Q).
 \label{eq:trace-integration}
\end{aligned}$$ Primes occurring only on returns need not appear in the product over tree or list primes. The $o(1)$ tends to zero as $B$ tends to infinity, uniformly in the line, list and record, with $h,\tau,C_0,T$ and the constants in <a href="#eq:graph-constants" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:graph-constants">[eq:graph-constants]</a> fixed.

Fix the topology, token shapes, tagged/list groupings and untagged multiplicities. For every non-skipped good origin $u\notin\mathcal U$ choose deterministically one named core slot whose token has top $u$. When summing prime assignments, the bin conditions on these edges give an extra factor $O(B^{-1+\eta})$ for each chosen slot, while retaining the unrestricted harmonic factor $v_a$ for every prime variable. Together with the charge exponent and its correction in <a href="#eq:trace-integration" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:trace-integration">[eq:trace-integration]</a>, these savings are bounded by $$\label{eq:trace-pivot-saving}
 B^{-100r_*}\exp(O(\ell))
       B^{-(1-\eta)(|\mathcal G|-N_{\mathrm{exc}})}.$$

Let $a\ge1$ be an integer and let $f\colon\mathbb N\to\mathbb D$ be multiplicative with $f(1)=1$. Define $$F_{a,f}(m)=
 \begin{cases}
 f(m/a),&a\mid m,\\
 0,&a\nmid m.
 \end{cases}
 \label{eq:affine-dilation-function}$$ Then $F_{a,f}$ is a signed sum of $2^{\omega(a)}$ normalized, $1$-bounded multiplicative functions, each agreeing with $f$ at every prime power whose prime does not divide $a$. If $f$ is uniformly nonpretentious, every function in this sum is uniformly nonpretentious.

Let $l\ge1$ and $0\le b<l$ be integers, and set $$d=\gcd(b,l),\qquad q=l/d,\qquad c=b/d.$$ For every positive integer $m$, $$\mathbf 1_{m\equiv b\pmod l}
 =\frac1{\varphi(q)}\sum_{\chi\bmod q}
       \overline{\chi(c)}F_{d,\chi}(m).
 \label{eq:affine-character-expansion}$$ Each $F_{d,\chi}$ is a signed sum of $2^{\omega(d)}$ normalized, $1$-bounded multiplicative functions agreeing with $\chi$ at all prime powers outside $d$. For $q=1$, the character in the formula is the constant function one, including at the residue $c=0$, and $\varphi(1)=1$.

Let $f_1,f_2\colon\mathbb N\to\mathbb D$ be multiplicative, with at least one uniformly nonpretentious. For fixed distinct nonnegative integers $h_1,h_2$, an integer $l\ge1$, and any integer $b$, $$\frac1X\sum_{1\le n\le X}
 f_1(n+h_1)f_2(n+h_2)\mathbf 1_{n\equiv b\pmod l}
 \longrightarrow0
 \qquad(X\longrightarrow\infty).
 \label{eq:affine-progression-limit}$$ The limit holds through all real $X$, including nonunit residue classes.
